WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.563 --> 00:00:03.380
(orca sounds)

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.470
- [Narrator] Orcas have
a language of their own.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.790
They communicate through touch, movement,

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.683
and most importantly, sound.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:11.960
And it's marine scientist

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.423
Ellyne Hamran's job to eavesdrop on them.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.070
She's an acoustic researcher studying

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.290
the sounds marine mammals

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.563
like whales and dolphins
use to communicate.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.810
She's captured hundreds of
hours of orca recordings,

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.210
like the one you're hearing.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.300
- Whales are using sound
as their primary sense

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.500
unlike humans that are using their vision.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.610
So, it's very important
for whales to be able

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.110
to communicate to each
other, to find mates,

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:47.110
to search for prey, also
to navigate the area.

00:00:47.112 --> 00:00:49.310
- [Narrator] This summer,
she's listening to the orcas

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.489
of Norway's Lofoten archipelago,

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:53.608
beloved by the creatures
for its healthy populations

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:56.580
of herring and other fish to feed on.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.590
But an invisible problem is
increasingly alarming experts,

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.900
noise pollution from
whale-watching cruises,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.323
oil exploration, and other human activity.

00:01:09.032 --> 00:01:12.320
Ellyne has set out to study
how it affects the whales

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.810
and she hopes to use her findings

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.550
to help make Lofoten a
marine protected area,

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:19.263
limiting unwelcome noise
and blocking oil drilling.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.860
She works for Ocean Sounds,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:28.020
a nonprofit that advocates for
marine ecosystem conservation

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.283
and has been tracking orcas
here in Lofoten since 2003.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.904
On a typical day, Ellyne,
her husband Bjorn,

00:01:34.904 --> 00:01:37.562
their dog Bailey, and
a team of researchers

00:01:37.562 --> 00:01:39.050
drive around the islands

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.303
in an inflatable boat looking for action.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.947
When they spot whales,
they slowly approach,

00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:48.013
then turn off the engine
to minimize noise.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.440
They use an underwater microphone

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.493
called a hydrophone to
record vocalizations.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.160
- During socializing, they're producing

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.960
a variety of vocalizations.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.150
It can be whistles, calls, buzzes, clicks,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.140
same with when they're feeding.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.063
They're a little bit more
quiet when they're traveling.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.340
So, we'll be able to go and record them

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.860
when we have the right moment.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.083
- [Crew Member] Breach, breach, breach!

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.150
- [Narrator] They also
photograph the orcas

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.190
to keep track of the family groups.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.130
The scientists assign
each of them a number

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.090
and add them to a catalog.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.808
- We will be able to use this method

00:02:28.808 --> 00:02:30.700
to track them over time.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.310
It's a very noninvasive

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.600
and inexpensive way of doing the research.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:37.880
- [Narrator] Bjorn operates a drone

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.613
that films whales from above.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.700
Over time, they match the
orcas' sounds up with behaviors,

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.780
gradually learning their complex language.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.280
Each pod speaks its own dialect,

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.860
so tracking them is essential

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.693
for understanding the communication.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.140
The large population of
whales also attracts thousands

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.850
of people looking for
boat tours every year.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:02.470
But these ships full of whale lovers

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:06.020
can actually be quite
disturbing to ocean life.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:10.030
- We've had issues of
boats zooming through pods,

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.520
sometimes even between
the mother and calf.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:15.333
It's also quite loud on the hydrophone,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:19.240
even loud enough that you
want to turn the volume down,

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.290
but the whales are not able to go in

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.880
and modify how loud the boats are.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.810
And I don't think people
are aware of this issue.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.000
- [Narrator] When a boat
drives too close to orcas,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.200
it can overpower their calls.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.090
- It could mask the vocalizations

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.710
so they can't be able to communicate

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:39.650
with each other nearby or find a mate.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.360
- [Narrator] And it can scare
away the fish orcas feed on.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.920
- [Ellyne] They spend quite a lot of time

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.000
rounding up the herring to feed

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.810
and then a boat could come way too close

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.623
and now disperse the fish.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.030
- [Narrator] The looming
threat of oil drilling

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.710
also poses a problem.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.680
In 2019, the Norwegian government reached

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.540
a deal to block drilling
in Lofoten for now.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.230
But every seat in the parliament

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.548
is up for election in September 2021,

00:04:04.548 --> 00:04:07.820
which could put the area at risk again.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.530
And even without permission to drill,

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:12.130
companies can still use
blasts of compressed air

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.340
to map where oil lies
beneath the ocean floor.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.470
These seismic surveys can be louder

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:21.190
to marine life than fireworks
from three feet away.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.210
Researchers aren't sure how these sounds

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.270
affect ocean mammals' health,

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.231
but studies have found
that a single air gun blast

00:04:27.231 --> 00:04:29.393
can be heard underwater for months.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.400
All this, combined with occasional

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.230
military exercises in the area,

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.030
creates an underwater cacophony

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:39.760
that interrupts whale communications.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.320
- They're vocalizing
already at the surface.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.253
So, we'll deploy the hydrophone.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.237
Just boat noise now,
they've stopped calling.

00:04:50.237 --> 00:04:51.487
- [Woman] Okay.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.470
- [Narrator] Because orcas are
instinctively collaborative,

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:58.650
communication is central
to everything they do.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.670
Disoriented whales may also swim out

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.680
of their usual living area.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.060
- [Ellyne] With the whales wanting

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.360
to be away from all the boat noise

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.930
and needing a bit more space,

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.180
sometimes they're hugged
a bit on the coastline,

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.380
too close to the rocks.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:13.596
- [Narrator] Ellyne says this may explain

00:05:13.596 --> 00:05:16.420
why the spring of 2020 saw more whales

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.770
stranded on the coasts
than any previous season.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:23.470
- It could be due to noise
pollution and acoustic trauma.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.997
If we were able to be here within

00:05:25.997 --> 00:05:29.379
the first 48 hours of
when the whales stranded

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:33.620
and to actually look into the head

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.400
and see if there's any damage
within the ear canal areas.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.130
- [Narrator] The strandings
could also have been caused

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:41.620
by a harmful algae bloom

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.460
or a virus that affected several species.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.560
But unless Ellyne and the team can examine

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.940
a stranded whale shortly after its death,

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.920
they can't gather much information.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.230
- It actually wasn't reported in time

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:56.340
to really see why it had died.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.820
We unfortunately cannot
take a blubber sample

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.880
since it's decayed too much.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.454
Usually at this point we take a tooth

00:06:03.454 --> 00:06:06.920
or we take a bone sample.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.470
And there were no teeth in the jaw,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.377
but we do have a sample of one

00:06:12.377 --> 00:06:14.423
of the vertebrae that we've taken.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:16.750
- [Narrator] Ellyne collected samples

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.040
from other stranded whales,

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.140
including a pilot whale,
during this research mission.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:22.880
These samples of blubber and bone

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.100
can tell researchers
about an animal's sex,

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:26.953
genetics, diet, and more.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:31.270
Ultimately, Ellyne's findings support

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:32.790
Ocean Sounds' efforts to educate

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.450
the public about protecting the mammals.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.780
- Our focus isn't only on research,

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.120
but we're also focused on conservation

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.630
and outreach and education.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:44.220
- [Narrator] Ocean Sounds
has a set of guidelines

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:45.400
for whale-watching boats that

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.160
they're lobbying to
turn into Norwegian law.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.398
They include requiring boats to slow down,

00:06:50.398 --> 00:06:53.100
shift into neutral, and
then turn off the engine

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.600
while approaching whales,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.400
and maintaining a minimum distance

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.800
of 50 meters at all times.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.220
The goal is to regulate
noise levels in the region

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.310
while still allowing whale-watching
and fishing to operate.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:06.600
And making Lofoten a marine protected area

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.810
would ban oil exploration
and drilling in the area.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:10.900
Until that happens,

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:12.500
Ocean Sounds is selling recordings

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.370
of whale communications online

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:17.850
as a way to raise awareness
and fund their operations.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.100
- We're trying to be able

00:07:19.998 --> 00:07:22.870
to not only study the
vocalizations and behavior,

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:26.070
but also to bring the
whales to the people,

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:27.943
giving a voice to the whales.

00:07:33.795 --> 00:07:36.378
(gentle music)

